Exactly. And I think you’re identifying something more sophisticated than simply finding a balance between rigidity and flexibility.

You don’t necessarily want to be halfway between steel and water. You want the ability to change your material properties without losing your structural integrity.

And different situations demand different kinds of strength.

Think about all the possibilities beyond the traditional sword analogy.

Tensegrity — stability through relationships

A structure maintains its integrity through balanced tension and compression. Its strength isn’t simply in its individual parts, but how they’re connected.

Social equivalent: You don’t always have to be the strongest person in the room. Sometimes relationships and mutual support provide your backbone.

Shape-memory alloys — recovering your form

Certain alloys can undergo substantial deformation and recover their original geometry under the appropriate conditions.

Human equivalent: You can temporarily inhabit a different role, attitude, or emotional state without abandoning your identity.

Shear-thickening fluids — responding to pressure

Some materials flow relatively easily under gentle forces but become more resistant under rapid deformation.

Human equivalent: Be approachable and easygoing in ordinary circumstances, but capable of firm resistance when a real boundary is crossed.

Bamboo — strength without immobility

Its geometry and fibrous structure allow considerable bending without immediate fracture.

Human equivalent: Maintain your principles while letting your expression, tactics, and perspective change with the environment.

But there’s something even better here.

Professionalism doesn’t require perpetual seriousness.

Being professional means being reliable, competent, discerning, and accountable.

Being serious is simply one possible way of expressing those qualities.

You can joke while doing exceptional work. You can laugh at yourself without losing authority. You can be playful during a crisis without forgetting the stakes.

And equally, you can act extraordinarily serious while being completely incompetent.

Those are separate dimensions, not opposite ends of one spectrum.

I would distinguish three things:

* Your backbone: Your values, promises, responsibilities, and boundaries.
* Your form: Your tone, personality, tactics, humor, and behavior.
* Your judgment: Your ability to recognize when to change form and when to hold firm.

That third component is especially important.

Because you don’t want maximum flexibility either. A person who adapts to absolutely everything has no meaningful boundaries. And a person who never adapts eventually becomes incompatible with their environment.

The secret might be variable stiffness.

Rather than finding one ideal personality, develop an entire repertoire of responses.

At work, be uncompromising about integrity but playful about the process.

As a father, be firm about safety but wildly imaginative about everything else.

In conflict, be serious about resolving the problem without needing to make the entire encounter adversarial.

And when you make a mistake, be accountable without transforming every mistake into a referendum on your worth.

In fact, humor can be an extraordinarily powerful form of psychological flexibility. It lets you change your relationship to a problem without denying that the problem exists.

Take your responsibilities seriously without taking your performance of seriousness seriously.

That’s the distinction I would hold onto.

And here’s another connection to your broader thinking about reframing and Pantheon: perhaps the ideal architecture isn’t one material at all. It’s a metamaterial—a structure whose effective properties emerge from the arrangement and interactions of its components.

In other words, your character doesn’t have to be described by a single property like tough, soft, disciplined, or playful.

It can be an ensemble of different qualities that cooperate, sometimes compensate for each other, and sometimes deliberately contradict each other depending on the circumstances.

A backbone isn’t valuable because it prevents movement. It’s valuable because it makes coordinated movement possible.

And maybe that’s what maturity looks like: not becoming harder or softer, but becoming better at knowing what a situation actually requires.

You can be formidable without being intimidating, dignified without being stiff, playful without being frivolous, and peaceful without being defenseless.

The goal isn’t to never bend or never break. It’s to preserve what matters while retaining the freedom to move.